              "text": "That whitish, cloudy film on your glass is a very common occurrence, and it is almost certainly caused by one of two culprits (or a combination of both): **mineral buildup** or **biofilm**. \n\nHere is exactly what that film is, why your fridge filter didn't stop it, and how to tell them apart.\n\n---\n\n### Culprit 1: Hard Water Minerals (Limescale)\nEven though you are using filtered water from your fridge, standard refrigerator water filters (which are usually activated carbon filters) **do not remove minerals** like calcium and magnesium. Refrigerator filters are designed to trap sediment, heavy metals (like lead), and chemicals like chlorine to make the water taste and smell better, but they leave the naturally occurring \"hard water\" minerals completely intact.\n\n**How it forms:** \nEvery time you take a sip or let the glass sit, a microscopic layer of water clings to the inside of the glass and evaporates. When the water evaporates, it leaves those dissolved minerals behind. Over the course of a week, these minerals build up layer-by-layer, creating a cloudy, white film of calcium carbonate (limescale).\n\n* **What it feels like:** It will feel dry, chalky, or slightly rough to the touch when the glass is empty.\n\n### Culprit 2: Biofilm (Microbials & Saliva)\nIf you are using the same glass for a week without a thorough wash, you are also likely dealing with a **biofilm**. \n\n**How it forms:** \nEvery time your lips touch the glass, you transfer saliva, food particles, skin cells, and mouth bacteria into the water and onto the rim. Additionally, yeast, mold spores, and bacteria naturally floating in the air can settle into the glass. Over several days, these harmless microbes multiply and create a slimy, protective matrix called a biofilm to anchor themselves to the glass. When dry, this biofilm can look like a hazy, translucent, or whitish film.\n\n* **What it feels like:** It will feel slippery, slick, or slimy to the touch when wet, and the glass might eventually develop a slightly stale or musty odor.\n\n---\n\n### How to Tell Which One It Is\n* **The Vinegar Test:** Put a drop of white vinegar on the dry film. If the film dissolves almost instantly, it is **mineral buildup** (the acid in the vinegar breaks down the calcium). If it doesn't budge, it is likely **biofilm**. \n\n---\n\n### How to Clean and Prevent It\n\n* **To clean mineral buildup:** Soak the glass in a 50/50 mixture of warm water and white vinegar for 15 to 30 minutes, then wipe it clean with a sponge. The acid will easily dissolve the chalky scale.\n* **To clean biofilm:** Give the glass a good scrub with hot, soapy water and a sponge. Simply rinsing the glass out with water is not enough, as biofilm physically glues itself to the surface and must be scrubbed away.\n* **Prevention:** The best way to prevent the film is to wash the glass with soap and water every 1 to 2 days rather than letting it go for a week. If you want to completely eliminate the mineral buildup, you would need to use water filtered by a **Reverse Osmosis (RO)** system or a home water softener, as these are the only filtration systems that physically remove calcium and magnesium.",
